# Line endings
#
# Makefiles must never reach the working tree with CRLF. make does not strip a
# carriage return, so a CRLF Makefile hands the linker "-lpthread\r" -- a
# library that does not exist. The error reads "cannot find -lpthread", because
# a CR is invisible in a terminal, and re-running the printed command by hand
# appears to succeed, because copying it through a shell drops the CR. That is
# a genuinely expensive hour, and it has already cost one.
#
# eol=lf pins the WORKING TREE, not just the blob, so core.autocrlf=true on a
# Windows checkout cannot reintroduce it. Shell scripts are here for the same
# reason: a CR after the shebang makes the interpreter unfindable.
Makefile        text eol=lf
*.mk            text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf

# C sources are LF everywhere for diff hygiene rather than correctness -- a
# CRLF .c file compiles fine, but it rewrites the whole file in every diff.
*.c             text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
